          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following best explains why a vegetarian Indian woman who drinks te</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following best explains why a vegetarian Indian woman who drinks tea with meals is at high risk for iron deficiency anaemia?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Vegetarian diets cause vitamin B12 deficiency, which impairs iron utilisation in the marrow</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Vitamin B12 deficiency causes megaloblastic anaemia, not iron deficiency. These are separate nutritional deficiencies with distinct mechanisms.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Her diet lacks haem iron and contains phytates and tannins that inhibit non-haem iron absorption</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Vegetarian diets provide only non-haem iron (2–5% absorption vs 35% for haem iron). Phytates in cereals and tannins in tea chelate iron in the gut, further reducing uptake.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Tea consumption increases urinary excretion of iron, depleting body stores</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Tannins in tea inhibit iron absorption in the gut lumen by chelating iron. They do not affect renal iron excretion — iron has no significant urinary excretion pathway.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Plant-based diets contain excess calcium that competitively displaces iron from transferrin binding sites</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>While calcium can modestly inhibit iron absorption, it does not displace iron from transferrin. Phytates and tannins are the dominant inhibitors in vegetarian diets.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Incorrect. Vegetarian diets provide only non-haem iron (2–5% absorption vs 35% for haem iron), and phytates and tannins in cereals and tea further chelate the little iron available."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The convergence of low haem iron intake, phytate-rich foods, and tannin-containing tea creates a triple barrier to iron absorption — a pattern common across much of India."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
