        {
          "duration": {
            "from": 349.62500000000006,
            "to": 359.62500000000006
          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In iron deficiency anaemia, ferrochelatase cannot insert iron into protoporphyri</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In iron deficiency anaemia, ferrochelatase cannot insert iron into protoporphyrin. Despite continued erythropoietin drive, each daughter cell makes less haemoglobin. Which laboratory finding is a direct consequence of this mechanism?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Macrocytic red cells with elevated mean corpuscular volume</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Macrocytosis results from impaired DNA synthesis, as seen in vitamin B12 or folate deficiency. Iron deficiency produces microcytosis because cells lack haemoglobin to fill them.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Elevated reticulocyte production index above 3</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The reticulocyte production index is low (below 2) in iron deficiency because erythropoiesis is iron-restricted. An elevated index indicates haemolysis or acute blood loss with adequate iron stores.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Microcytic hypochromic red cells with expanded central pallor</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>When ferrochelatase lacks iron, haemoglobin synthesis is incomplete. Cells remain small (MCV below 80 fL) and pale (MCHC below 31 g/dL) because there is insufficient haemoglobin to fill and colour them.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Normal red cell morphology with isolated low serum ferritin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Normal morphology with low ferritin represents early storage depletion (stage 1) before anaemia develops. Once ferrochelatase is limited, red cell morphology is always abnormal.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Incorrect. Insufficient iron for ferrochelatase means cells cannot make enough haemoglobin, producing small (microcytic) and pale (hypochromic) red cells with expanded central pallor."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The ferrochelatase block produces under-filled, under-coloured red cells — the hallmark microcytic hypochromic picture of iron deficiency on the peripheral smear."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
