          "label": "<p>Which triad defines Plummer-Vinson syndrome?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which triad defines Plummer-Vinson syndrome?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Megaloblastic anaemia, glossitis, and peripheral neuropathy</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This triad suggests vitamin B12 deficiency with subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord, not iron deficiency.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Iron deficiency anaemia, postcricoid dysphagia, and oesophageal webs</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Plummer-Vinson syndrome is this specific triad. It carries a 10–15% risk of squamous cell carcinoma of the pharynx or upper oesophagus and is therefore premalignant.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Iron deficiency anaemia, koilonychia, and atrophic glossitis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Koilonychia and atrophic glossitis are iron-specific epithelial signs, but they are not part of the Plummer-Vinson triad. The triad specifically includes postcricoid dysphagia and oesophageal webs.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Haemolytic anaemia, jaundice, and splenomegaly</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>This triad suggests haemolytic anaemia with extravascular haemolysis, not iron deficiency. Plummer-Vinson is exclusive to iron deficiency anaemia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Incorrect. Plummer-Vinson syndrome is the triad of iron deficiency anaemia, postcricoid dysphagia, and oesophageal webs. It is premalignant, with a 10–15% risk of pharyngeal or upper oesophageal squamous cell carcinoma."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Plummer-Vinson syndrome is a premalignant triad — always investigate further because of the 10–15% squamous carcinoma risk."
                }
              ],
